course: dictionary
lesson: 12334
title: Courteously: polite and kind
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: "Courteously" comes from the word "courteous", which means polite and kind. The "-ly" at the end tells you that the word describes how someone does something. It is an adverb, so it tells you the way an action happens.
explain: «Sam courteously held the door open for the old man.» Holding a door open is a kind thing to do. The word courteously tells you Sam did it politely and with good manners. Try swapping in "rudely": the sentence no longer fits, because Sam was not behaving badly.
explain: Stories often use courteously to show that a character behaves well even when others do not. «The angry customer shouted, but the shop assistant answered courteously.» The word "but" is a clue: the shouting is rude, so courteously must mean the opposite, politely and calmly.
fact: "Courteously" means in a polite, kind and respectful way.
fact: "Courteous" is the adjective, and "courteously" is the adverb that tells you how someone does something.
fact: You can use courteously for good manners, such as speaking to a teacher, a shop assistant or a bus driver.
fact: In a formal sense, courteously means in a correct and considerate way, as officials and shop staff are expected to behave.
fact: Phrases you may see are «speak courteously», «reply courteously», «treat someone courteously» and «courteously declined».
exercise: «The shop assistant spoke courteously to every customer.» What does courteously mean? | In a sleepy way | In a great hurry | In a loud, angry way | *In a polite, kind way
exercise: «The bus driver courteously waited for the children to sit down.» Find and copy the word that shows the driver was being polite. | *courteously | children | driver | waited
exercise: "Courteous" is the adjective. Which word is the adverb that goes with it? | courteous | *courteously | courtesy | court
test: «Ella replied courteously, "No thanks, I'm fine."» How did Ella reply? | *Politely and with good manners | Whispering a secret | Rudely and loudly | Crying and upset
test: «Even though the boys laughed at him, Tom courteously declined their invitation.» Why does courteously fit here? | Because Tom was very tired | Because Tom shouted back at them | Because Tom was late | *Because Tom stayed polite even though they were unkind
exam: Mrs Patel says, "Please always treat visitors courteously." What should you do? | Run away from them | Ignore them | *Speak to them politely and kindly | Shout at them
